WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.440
Not every parameter carries the same weight. Some fields are essential for the tool to function at

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:11.760
all. Others add useful context when it's available. In this video, we'll cover required and optional

3
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:18.639
parameters, how enum fields constrain Claude to a fixed set of values, where input examples help,

4
00:00:18.639 --> 00:00:25.840
and how bounds finish a reliable schema. Every field you put in the required array is a promise

5
00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:31.920
that Claude can always supply it. So let's lock down three definitions. A required parameter is

6
00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:39.040
any field listed in that top level required array. Claude has to include it in every call,

7
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:45.680
and if its generated arguments are missing one, the call fails schema validation before your tool

8
00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:53.919
code ever runs. An optional parameter is any field not in that array. Claude may omit it when the

9
00:00:53.919 --> 00:00:59.840
information isn't available, and your tool code has to be ready for that. A default value is the

10
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:06.160
fallback that applies when an optional parameter is omitted. Defaults can be declared in the schema

11
00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:12.720
itself or handled inside the tool implementation. Using defaults well is what makes optional

12
00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:19.360
parameters safe rather than fragile. Here's a design tension worth understanding. Every field

13
00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:25.680
you put in your required array increases the chance that a tool call will fail when Claude is

14
00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:31.360
working in a context where that information isn't available. When Claude can't find a value for a

15
00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:37.599
required field, it sometimes hallucinates one to satisfy the schema. That's worse than a failed

16
00:01:37.599 --> 00:01:43.680
call because the hallucinated value looks valid but isn't. The discipline here is to only mark a

17
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:49.279
field required if the tool genuinely can't function without it. If the tool can produce a

18
00:01:49.279 --> 00:01:56.400
sensible result with a default or skip the field entirely, make it optional. A simple decision

19
00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.680
framework helps when you're not sure whether a field should be required or optional. If the tool

20
00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:06.800
can't function without it, mark it required and accept that the call should fail fast when the

21
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:12.559
value is missing. If the field is useful but not critical, make it optional and declare a sensible

22
00:02:12.559 --> 00:02:18.559
default in your implementation so the tool still produces a good result. If the value is rarely

23
00:02:18.559 --> 00:02:25.199
provided, make it optional and handle its absence explicitly in your tool code. This framework keeps

24
00:02:25.199 --> 00:02:30.880
your required set lean, which means Claude can call your tool successfully across a wider range

25
00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:38.000
of contexts without resorting to hallucination. When a parameter accepts only a fixed set of values,

26
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:44.399
an enum constraint is exactly the right tool. You declare enum as an array of the acceptable values

27
00:02:44.399 --> 00:02:50.639
for that field, and Claude picks from that list instead of generating a free-form string. Under

28
00:02:50.639 --> 00:02:56.080
strict tool use, the model is guaranteed to stay inside the set. That's especially powerful for

29
00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:02.000
routing parameters like a priority field that sends requests to different handlers. A couple

30
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:07.919
of related constraints round this out. Minimum and maximum apply numeric bounds to integer and

31
00:03:07.919 --> 00:03:14.960
number fields, which matters for page sizes, scores, and item counts, where an out-of-range value

32
00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:20.720
breaks something downstream. And additional properties set to false declares that no

33
00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:26.240
undeclared fields are allowed, which strict tool use enforces so Claude can't invent extras.

34
00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:33.600
The contrast between a free-form string and an enum field makes the value concrete. Without an

35
00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:40.399
enum, a priority field declared as type string gives Claude unlimited options. It might generate

36
00:03:40.399 --> 00:03:46.720
urgent, high priority, urgent, critical, or any other variation depending on whatever context

37
00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:52.240
happens to be in the conversation at that moment. All of those variations may break the logic that

38
00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:58.240
branches on the value because your code is almost certainly checking for exact string matches.

39
00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:04.800
Add enum, low, medium, high, and Claude has to pick one of those three exact values every

40
00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:10.479
single time, regardless of context. The field becomes deterministic and predictable.

41
00:04:10.479 --> 00:04:16.239
Your back-end code gets simpler and more reliable as a direct result of that single schema change.

42
00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:23.279
Enum fields show up in two main patterns in real-world tool schemas. The first is routing

43
00:04:23.279 --> 00:04:30.160
inputs, where the enum value directs the tool call to a specific handler, workflow, or service.

44
00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:36.720
The second is extraction fields, where Claude is classifying an entity or event into a predefined

45
00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:42.640
category, like a sentiment label or a document type. In both cases, the valid set has to be

46
00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:48.880
stable and reasonably small. Enums work well for two to ten values that don't change often.

47
00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:54.399
If your valid set has hundreds of members or changes frequently, enum isn't the right

48
00:04:54.399 --> 00:05:00.079
constraint and you'll want a different validation approach. Constraints tighten what Claude can

49
00:05:00.079 --> 00:05:06.799
generate, but sometimes a valid shape still isn't an obvious usage pattern. That's what the input

50
00:05:06.799 --> 00:05:13.440
examples field is for. You add an optional input examples array to the tool definition, holding a

51
00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:19.839
few sample inputs, and each one has to be valid against your input schema or the whole request is

52
00:05:19.839 --> 00:05:26.079
rejected. Descriptions are still the most important lever, so reach for examples when a tool has

53
00:05:26.079 --> 00:05:32.959
nested objects, a lot of optional fields, or format-sensitive parameters where valid JSON

54
00:05:32.959 --> 00:05:39.359
doesn't guarantee correct usage. Examples measurably improve accuracy on complex parameter

55
00:05:39.359 --> 00:05:44.959
handling. They cost a few extra prompt tokens and they aren't supported on server tools,

56
00:05:44.959 --> 00:05:49.440
but on genuinely complex tools, they earn their place in the definition.

57
00:05:50.959 --> 00:05:56.480
Let's pull this together. Only mark fields required when the tool genuinely can't function

58
00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:02.559
without them. Give optional fields sensible defaults and use enum constraints to eliminate

59
00:06:02.559 --> 00:06:09.279
freeform string variation for any fixed value input. Numeric bounds and additional properties

60
00:06:09.279 --> 00:06:14.480
tighten the rest. Those decisions are what separate a schema that Claude can satisfy every

61
00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:20.799
time from one that quietly produces bad calls. That's it for this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

